WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:03.570
- I am also witnessing many,
many of my friends leaving.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:08.575
I just drove back from the
airport earlier this morning.

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:10.677
Saw about five or
six moving trucks.

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:15.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm kinda counting them
now, because I had a friend

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:18.051
come over last night tell
me he's moving to Florida.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:22.856 align:start position:0%
These are basically, upper
middle class and wealthy people.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:24.624
They've attached
base to the city

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.526 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I've never wonder
about you know,

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:28.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
since maybe I stopped
being a liberal,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.868
I always ask 'em, why do you
attack rich people so much?

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:38.271 align:start position:0%
Don't you realize you need them
to pay for the poor people?

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:42.075
(soothing instrumental music)

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
- This is the Rubin Report
and I'm David Rubin.

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:51.418 line:0%
Friendly reminder everybody,
to subscribe to our YouTube

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:53.553 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
channel and click
that notification bell

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.089 line:0%
so you're alerted when
new videos come out.

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:58.958 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Joining me today is the
former mayor of New York City

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:02.796 line:0%
and a special guest star of
one of the best ever episodes

00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:05.031 align:start size:81% position:19%
of Seinfeld, Mayor
Rudy Giuliani.

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:06.499
Welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.835
- Thank you very much Dave.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:11.404
- So I could basically spend

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:16.342
an hour talking to you about
Seinfeld and stuff you did

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:20.046 line:0%
on Saturday Live.
- I know we could.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.682 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- I expect there are probably
some more pressing things

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:23.883 line:0%
that you want to get to.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.418 line:0%
But I thought what
would be interesting,

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.288 line:0%
instead of just going into
all of the stuff of today,

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:31.191 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
which we will get to, is that
especially for my younger

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:33.626
audience on YouTube,
that sort of know you

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:38.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
as New York City's mayor and
911, that version of you,

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:42.368 align:start position:0%
you had a whole life and a
political evolution before that

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:45.438
that in many ways is sort of
like my political evolution.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:47.874 align:start size:88% position:13%
You fought off the mafia
as attorney general

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.275
and a whole bunch of stuff.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:52.479
So can we just do sort
of Rudy Giuliani 101.

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:55.915 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, really from childhood
on and then we'll get

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:56.950
to the topics of the day.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.485 line:0%
- Sure, I'll do it real quick.

00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:03.189 line:0%
I was born in Brooklyn, second
generation Italian American.

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:07.961 align:start size:84% position:16%
I went to a Catholic
grammar school.

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.796 align:start size:88% position:13%
Then I moved from
Brooklyn to Long Island,

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:13.199
which was quite common in
those days and continued

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.567
in Catholic grammar school.

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:17.370
- What town in Long
Island were you in?

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.006
- Yeah, in Garden City and
North Bellmore Long Island,

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:22.041 align:start size:84% position:16%
two different places
in Long Island.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.309
But I went to high
school in Brooklyn,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.577
Bishop Loughlin High School,

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.747
which was a special
dioceses school.

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:30.683
In part because I wanted to
be a priest in my early days.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.187
Kind of gave that
up when I found out

00:02:34.187 --> 00:02:38.892 align:start size:84% position:16%
there were things
called girls. (laughs)

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:41.094
And kind of didn't work
with the priesthood.

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:45.465
Then I went to college in
the Bronx in Riverdale,

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.767
Manhattan College and
law school at NYU.

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.371
Graduated law school
and became a law clerk

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:54.374 align:start size:88% position:13%
to a really fine federal
judge, Lloyd McMahon,

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:56.309
who taught me how to
be a trial lawyer.

00:02:57.477 --> 00:02:58.778 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then I entered the
U.S. Attorney's Office

00:02:58.778 --> 00:02:59.879
at a very young age.

00:03:01.281 --> 00:03:05.418 align:start position:0%
Because of his training, I sort
of started off as already a

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:08.221
pretty experienced trial lawyer

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:13.459 align:start size:97% position:3%
because he was a nice enough
man to kind of teach you

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.195
what you have to do
in the courtroom.

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.163
I loved being a trial lawyer.

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:20.200
I spent about five and
a half years doing that.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.001
Tried a 100 cases or so.

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.972
Some mafia cases,
bank robbery cases,

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:31.477
white collar cases,
income tax evasion,

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.079
customs fraud, just about
everything you can think of

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:35.515
that are federal crimes.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:40.086
Then I was the head of
the narcotics division,

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.188
which in those days
we focused a lot on

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:48.294
the French Connection, the
Italian French heroine trade,

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:52.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Columbian trade, all
the Columbian cartel people.

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:55.001
That was the first
time that somebody

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:57.303
put out a contract to kill me.

00:03:57.303 --> 00:04:00.240
Vito Genovese's brother in law

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
threatened to have me killed
because I prosecuted him

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.278
and he got convicted.

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:10.383 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Columbian cartel
threatened to slit my throat.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:14.721 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I was like 25, 26
years old and you don't,

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:19.959
I don't know, you feel
immortal at a young age.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.362 align:start size:97% position:3%
Then I went off to
Washington to be the deputy,

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.431 align:start size:88% position:13%
chief of staff to the
deputy attorney general,

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.834
Harold Tyler in the
Ford administration.

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.471 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's where I started
to think about,

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:34.173
I had left the democratic party

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:37.143
as an assistant U.S. attorney
because I was investigating

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:39.879
a lot of corruption
in city government

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.981
under John Lindsey.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:43.916 align:start size:88% position:13%
Even though John
Lindsey was a republican

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
who turned democrat, all the
people in city government

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:50.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
were democrats and all these
great society programs,

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:52.392 align:start size:84% position:16%
which I kinda
thought sounded nice,

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:54.894
it looked like none of it was
getting to the poor people.

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:57.530
It was all in the hands of the
administrator of the program,

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.367 align:start size:97% position:3%
his wife, some of the
crooked minority politicians

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.802
in the city,

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
who I quickly came to realize,
didn't really care about

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:07.874 align:start size:88% position:13%
their community, just
cared about getting rich

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:08.708
for themselves.

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.277
Very disillusioned.

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:14.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
I changed my registration
from democrat to independent.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.882
Then I went off and I
worked for two years

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:17.650
in the Ford administration.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.786 align:start size:88% position:13%
Met a lot of republicans
and found out

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:22.288
that they really
weren't terrible.

00:05:23.456 --> 00:05:25.692 align:start size:84% position:16%
My impression from New
York were you know,

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:27.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
republicans only care
about rich people

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.230
and they're really very
mean and turned out,

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.966 align:start size:88% position:13%
they were just as caring
about poor people.

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:36.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
They just had a different
way of trying to move

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:37.403
them out of poverty.

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:40.340
Kinda made more sense to
me that you move people

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.408 align:start size:84% position:16%
out of poverty through
work than welfare.

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:46.179
I kinda had always a strong
aggressive foreign policy

00:05:46.179 --> 00:05:47.880
with regard to Soviets,

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.316 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I always thought
of a Kennedy democrat.

00:05:50.316 --> 00:05:52.485
In those days, there was a
fellow named Stu Jackson,

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:55.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
a senator, who was a very
hard-liner on foreign policy.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.558
So then I switched to
being a republican.

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:00.993
I said well, let me try
the republican party.

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:05.498
Went into law practice, worked
for Ronald Reagan's election.

00:06:05.498 --> 00:06:07.767
Ronald Reagan made me the
third ranking official

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.068
in the justice department.

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:11.771
I did that, I came
back to New York,

00:06:11.771 --> 00:06:15.274
I became the U.S. attorney, I
prosecuted the five families

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.844
of the mafia, over
800 mafia people,

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:23.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
a large number of
Wall Street criminals,

00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:27.453
Boesky probably being the
most famous one, Milken,

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:32.625 align:start size:97% position:3%
prosecuted three or four
sitting congressmen, and also

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.795 align:start size:97% position:3%
started using the
racketeering statute in a way

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:37.497
that it hadn't been used before,

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:39.399
to break up organized crime.

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:41.634
We took over the
Teamsters Union,

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.136
drove out organized crime.

00:06:43.136 --> 00:06:46.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
We took over the casinos in
Las Vegas and got rid of them.

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:48.207
We took over the
Fulton Fish Market,

00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:53.613
the whole industry of
the garment industry

00:06:53.613 --> 00:06:55.014
and entertainment industry.

00:06:56.382 --> 00:06:59.685 align:start position:0%
Strangely, the mafia controlled
the gay bars in New York.

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:03.756
A combination of our work and
then the advent of HIV/AIDS

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:07.560
got rid of those places, but
the mafia owned those bars

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.861
for purposes of extortion.

00:07:09.796 --> 00:07:11.297
They owned every single one.

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:14.600
You couldn't open a gay bar
in New York and not have

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.904
Vito Genovese and
his number two guy

00:07:17.904 --> 00:07:21.274
walk in on you and tell you
that he owned half the bar.

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:24.143
You'd say, "Why," and they'd say

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:28.181
"Because if we don't
we break your kneecap."

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:31.484
So we got them out of there.

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:34.520
I had a very--

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.391
Then I guess, the thing that
made me famous as the mayor

00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:41.194
was a prosecuted a number
of the high level officials

00:07:41.194 --> 00:07:42.695
in the Koch administration.

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:46.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
His chief political allies
and proved that they

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:50.436 align:start position:0%
were involved in a multi-million
dollar bribery scheme

00:07:50.436 --> 00:07:55.441 align:start size:97% position:3%
to collect fines and divert
them to their own pocketbooks,

00:07:56.909 --> 00:07:58.811
to the tune of about 15 or
20 million dollars a year.

00:07:59.745 --> 00:08:00.980
- [Dave] Geez.

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.715
- That gave me a great
deal of name recognition

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.285 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I got the crazy idea
of running for mayor.

00:08:07.386 --> 00:08:08.988 align:start size:84% position:16%
All my friends
told me I was nuts.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:10.389
A republican couldn't win.

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.860 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought maybe I was nuts,
but I was young enough

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
to try to be nuts and find
out if I would enjoy politics.

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:20.166
I was lucky enough
to get David Garth

00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:22.268
as my political advisor.

00:08:23.636 --> 00:08:26.472
I started off at
27 points behind.

00:08:26.472 --> 00:08:27.607
I lost by two.

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:31.777
David taught me how to
be a political candidate.

00:08:31.777 --> 00:08:34.680 align:start position:0%
It was the best showing a
republican had made in 40 years.

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:37.283
Since LaGuardia.

00:08:37.283 --> 00:08:39.185
I got 48% of the vote.

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:42.455 align:start size:88% position:13%
I guess if I had gotten
20 I'd a given up,

00:08:42.455 --> 00:08:44.590 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
but 48% of the vote,
I said to myself,

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.093 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
maybe I can have another
chance to do this.

00:08:47.093 --> 00:08:49.996 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- Then you eventually,
you decided to do it again

00:08:49.996 --> 00:08:51.197 line:0%
and the rest is history.

00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:53.199 line:0%
- Four years later,
four years later

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:56.736
I did it again and I won by a
very narrow margin, about 3%.

00:08:58.037 --> 00:08:59.805 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then I ran for reelection
four years later

00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:01.207
and I won by a landslide.

00:09:02.275 --> 00:09:03.576
I was very fortunate.

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:05.778
I made a lot of promises.

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:08.948
I really went into the
job with the notion that

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:12.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
this is the first republican
in two generations.

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:14.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
I might be the last
republican mayor of New York.

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:20.660
Why not just do everything
I wanted to do and if I,

00:09:21.861 --> 00:09:23.930
if it worked, I'd be a
hero and if it didn't,

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.599
they'd throw me out and
maybe 20 years later

00:09:26.599 --> 00:09:29.068 align:start size:97% position:3%
they'd realize I was trying
to do the right thing.

00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:30.736
I really had that attitude.

00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:33.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I cut city government,
I cut city government

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:36.909
to what it should
be, by about 20%.

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:39.679
I laid off enormous
numbers of people.

00:09:39.679 --> 00:09:42.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
I decreased everything in
city government interestingly,

00:09:42.949 --> 00:09:46.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the police
department, because I had,

00:09:47.853 --> 00:09:49.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had a couple years
right before I was mayor,

00:09:49.956 --> 00:09:51.691
we had 2,200 murders.

00:09:53.492 --> 00:09:57.430 align:start size:88% position:13%
We had about 1.1 million
people on welfare.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.865
We had 10.5% unemployment.

00:09:59.865 --> 00:10:04.870 align:start size:97% position:3%
We had a deficit that was
said to be about 700 million.

00:10:05.738 --> 00:10:05.871
It really was 1.4 billion.

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:09.442 align:start size:88% position:13%
We had a situation
where 75% of the people

00:10:09.442 --> 00:10:11.243
in the city wanted to leave.

00:10:12.745 --> 00:10:16.315
So I ran on very much the
thing that Donald Trump

00:10:16.315 --> 00:10:19.018 align:start size:84% position:16%
used four years ago,
which I might say

00:10:19.018 --> 00:10:23.089
I kinda suggested he use, I
said you can't do any worse.

00:10:24.223 --> 00:10:27.326 align:start size:84% position:16%
How can New York City
be any worse than

00:10:27.326 --> 00:10:29.829
leading the country
in crime, murder,

00:10:31.497 --> 00:10:36.335 align:start size:97% position:3%
violent crime, white collar
crime, organized crime, drugs?

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:38.270
We were ahead in everything.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
How can we be any
worse than you know,

00:10:42.575 --> 00:10:44.810
1.1 million people not working,
being supported by the other

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:45.945
people who are working?

00:10:47.413 --> 00:10:50.116
No Fortune 500 company in 20
years had come to New York

00:10:50.116 --> 00:10:51.550
and about 20 had moved out.

00:10:53.085 --> 00:10:56.088
Our tax base was getting shrunk.

00:10:56.088 --> 00:10:58.758
Even movies wouldn't be
made in New York anymore.

00:10:58.758 --> 00:11:01.494
They were making the New
York movies in Toronto

00:11:01.494 --> 00:11:03.996 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they were too
expensive to make in New York

00:11:03.996 --> 00:11:04.897
and too dangerous.

00:11:06.165 --> 00:11:08.034
So I said give me a chance.

00:11:08.034 --> 00:11:10.036 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know everything's
wrong with it.

00:11:10.036 --> 00:11:11.237 align:start size:84% position:16%
I have 12 years
experience of putting

00:11:11.237 --> 00:11:13.172
all the bad guys in jail.

00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:16.208 align:start size:97% position:3%
Maybe I can turn the knowledge
of everything that's wrong

00:11:16.208 --> 00:11:17.910 align:start size:84% position:16%
with it into
straightening it out.

00:11:19.378 --> 00:11:22.481
- All right, there's whole
bunch of off-shoots there.

00:11:22.481 --> 00:11:24.917
- Very specific
plans to do that.

00:11:24.917 --> 00:11:26.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah all right, so there's
a couple different ways

00:11:26.452 --> 00:11:27.953
we could go there.

00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:30.089
So first off, I mentioned to
you when I was on your podcast

00:11:30.089 --> 00:11:32.124
a couple days ago, we have
a sort of similar history.

00:11:32.124 --> 00:11:34.126 align:start size:88% position:13%
Not only because of
the political evolution

00:11:34.126 --> 00:11:36.796 align:start size:97% position:3%
of sort of democrat to
independent to what you know,

00:11:36.796 --> 00:11:38.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would say I'm sort of
like a modern conservative

00:11:38.731 --> 00:11:41.067 align:start size:97% position:3%
at this point, which I think
is sort of what you are.

00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:43.335
Even we're sort of
in the same spot,

00:11:43.335 --> 00:11:46.338
even on abortion, which is not
thought of as a conservative

00:11:46.338 --> 00:11:47.973
position, specifically.

00:11:47.973 --> 00:11:50.843 align:start position:0%
But the guy whose name you
actually didn't mention in there

00:11:50.843 --> 00:11:52.611
was the guy that you
replaced as mayor,

00:11:52.611 --> 00:11:55.314
which was David Dinkins, who
was a far left progressive.

00:11:55.314 --> 00:11:59.051
He basically is the one that
sort of wrecked New York City

00:11:59.051 --> 00:12:01.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I remember going
to New York City.

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:03.189
My grandparents lived there

00:12:03.189 --> 00:12:05.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
and my great
grandparents lived there.

00:12:05.591 --> 00:12:07.693
You couldn't go to Times Square.

00:12:07.693 --> 00:12:10.896
You cleaned all of that stuff up

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:12.765 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think a lot of
people right now are seeing

00:12:12.765 --> 00:12:15.901 align:start size:97% position:3%
what de Blasio is doing
with many of the same ideas

00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:18.304
as David Dinkins and
all these you know,

00:12:18.304 --> 00:12:20.773 align:start position:0%
the big government progressives,
they're seeing that.

00:12:20.773 --> 00:12:23.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
I do sense that maybe
there's another Rudy Giuliani

00:12:23.709 --> 00:12:24.944
in the waiting.

00:12:24.944 --> 00:12:26.412
But I gotta back up
to something else

00:12:26.412 --> 00:12:29.615 align:start size:97% position:3%
because you're a second
generation Italian American

00:12:29.615 --> 00:12:31.617
and you went after the mafia.

00:12:31.617 --> 00:12:33.519
So you gotta explain
that a little bit more.

00:12:33.519 --> 00:12:36.722 align:start size:97% position:3%
How did that, just in
terms of your own ethnicity

00:12:36.722 --> 00:12:38.624
and identity, how is that?

00:12:39.925 --> 00:12:43.095
- Well, you know I,

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:45.831
I knew the mafia from
growing up in Brooklyn

00:12:45.831 --> 00:12:48.601
and from being an
Italian family.

00:12:48.601 --> 00:12:51.437 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was not one of those people
where you had to tell me

00:12:51.437 --> 00:12:52.538
you know, what is the mafia.

00:12:52.538 --> 00:12:54.173
I knew what it was.

00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:57.276
I knew its history
from the Black Hand,

00:12:57.276 --> 00:12:59.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
when the Italian American
immigrants first came

00:12:59.812 --> 00:13:01.881
to this country at the
turn of the century.

00:13:01.881 --> 00:13:04.950 align:start size:84% position:16%
The Black Hand
would prey on them.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.453
I remember stories in my
family that my grandfather,

00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:10.823
who ran a large, large
tavern in Coney Island,

00:13:10.823 --> 00:13:13.592
several times he was

00:13:15.961 --> 00:13:17.563 align:start size:84% position:16%
required to pay
money to the mafia.

00:13:17.563 --> 00:13:20.099
Once when he didn't, they
burned part of his store.

00:13:21.667 --> 00:13:24.804
I was told what bullies they
are, how terrible they are.

00:13:24.804 --> 00:13:27.106
I had an impression of them

00:13:27.106 --> 00:13:30.042
as being very bad for
the Italian people.

00:13:31.243 --> 00:13:34.313
Then obviously found
out, as I got older,

00:13:34.313 --> 00:13:35.915
that there was also a
tremendous prejudice

00:13:35.915 --> 00:13:38.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
that every Italian
was part of the mafia.

00:13:38.617 --> 00:13:39.885
Including at times,

00:13:39.885 --> 00:13:42.054
I would be told that I
was part of the mafia.

00:13:43.255 --> 00:13:46.025
So it created both
sets of problems.

00:13:47.459 --> 00:13:49.228
When I became an assist
to United States attorney

00:13:49.228 --> 00:13:50.896
it wasn't really to
pursue the mafia,

00:13:50.896 --> 00:13:52.097
it was to be a trial lawyer.

00:13:52.097 --> 00:13:54.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's what I always
wanted to be all my life

00:13:54.366 --> 00:13:56.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
and try all different
kinds of crimes,

00:13:56.702 --> 00:13:58.771
but it just so happened that

00:13:58.771 --> 00:14:01.974
that was the era in which
the FBI was just beginning

00:14:01.974 --> 00:14:04.143
to really go after the mafia.

00:14:04.143 --> 00:14:06.979 align:start position:0%
J Edgar Hoover had always
preferred to go after communists

00:14:06.979 --> 00:14:07.813
than the mafia.

00:14:09.014 --> 00:14:11.617
I tried one big mafia
case as a young lawyer

00:14:11.617 --> 00:14:16.488
against a fellow
named Vito Gazia.

00:14:16.488 --> 00:14:20.259
His name was actually,
his nickname was Peter.

00:14:20.259 --> 00:14:21.727
They all have nicknames.

00:14:21.727 --> 00:14:23.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
He was the son in
law of Vito Genovese.

00:14:25.331 --> 00:14:27.099 align:start size:97% position:3%
We were allowed to prosecute
him because he cheated

00:14:27.099 --> 00:14:29.068
on Vito's sister.

00:14:30.269 --> 00:14:31.804
So Vito basically
let the witnesses,

00:14:31.804 --> 00:14:34.907 align:start size:97% position:3%
gave witnesses the permission
to testify against him.

00:14:34.907 --> 00:14:37.042 align:start size:88% position:13%
I had no idea why I
had all these witnesses

00:14:37.042 --> 00:14:39.612
testifying against
this big mafia guy.

00:14:39.612 --> 00:14:42.848
In the middle of the trial
he threatened to kill me,

00:14:42.848 --> 00:14:45.017
which because he was
a little bit nuts.

00:14:46.185 --> 00:14:49.054
So for the first time I
hadda have protection.

00:14:49.054 --> 00:14:51.557
I convicted him and it
was a big conviction.

00:14:52.925 --> 00:14:57.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I think I had that
conviction, thanks to Vito,

00:14:57.263 --> 00:14:59.131
who was willing to
tell three or four

00:14:59.131 --> 00:15:01.834 align:start size:94% position:6%
of his loan shark victims,
it's okay to testify

00:15:01.834 --> 00:15:03.369
against my brother in law

00:15:03.369 --> 00:15:06.906 align:start size:97% position:3%
because he's screwing around
on my sister. (laughs)

00:15:08.607 --> 00:15:10.943 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then I left, I
went to Washington.

00:15:12.878 --> 00:15:15.681
I was one of the people that
help establish the FISA report.

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:18.784
So when we litigate all this
stuff about the FISA court,

00:15:18.784 --> 00:15:22.655
Joe de Geneva, who you
see on television and I,

00:15:22.655 --> 00:15:25.391 align:start size:97% position:3%
were the two lawyers who
helped develop the FISA court.

00:15:26.692 --> 00:15:28.761
I dealt with the
church committee.

00:15:28.761 --> 00:15:30.262
I learned Washington,
came back to New York,

00:15:30.262 --> 00:15:32.631
I was in the law practice.

00:15:32.631 --> 00:15:34.133 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then I became the
third ranking official

00:15:34.133 --> 00:15:36.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the justice department
under Ronald Reagan,

00:15:36.568 --> 00:15:40.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's where I really
became interested in politics.

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:44.710
Because Reagan was to
me, bigger than life.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:49.014
Just a great, great man,
just a natural president,

00:15:49.014 --> 00:15:49.982
natural leader

00:15:51.283 --> 00:15:54.620 align:start position:0%
and unbelievably underestimated,
much like our present

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:56.522
president and a lot
of his success came

00:15:56.522 --> 00:15:58.524
from being underestimated.

00:15:58.524 --> 00:16:00.926
- Yeah, so we'll get to
our current president

00:16:00.926 --> 00:16:02.127
in a little bit.

00:16:02.127 --> 00:16:04.863 align:start size:84% position:16%
Obviously, you've
talked about you know,

00:16:04.863 --> 00:16:07.066
what 911 was like,
many, many times.

00:16:07.066 --> 00:16:09.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
So maybe sort of briefly,
you know I told you

00:16:09.902 --> 00:16:11.537
this the other day, I
lived in New York City

00:16:11.537 --> 00:16:13.806
on the Upper East
Side during 911.

00:16:13.806 --> 00:16:16.408
My dad worked in the garment
center, he saw the second plane

00:16:16.408 --> 00:16:19.578 align:start size:97% position:3%
hit, I had my grandma lived
there, plenty of family there.

00:16:19.578 --> 00:16:21.981
We were all there and
truly one of the things

00:16:21.981 --> 00:16:24.183
that I remember most you know,

00:16:24.183 --> 00:16:28.087 align:start size:97% position:3%
beyond the tangible stuff,
the smell that you could smell

00:16:28.087 --> 00:16:30.089
for weeks and all of that,

00:16:31.223 --> 00:16:35.427 align:start size:81% position:19%
is that you calmed
everybody down

00:16:35.427 --> 00:16:39.398
and showed actual true
leadership in a time

00:16:39.398 --> 00:16:42.201
when we needed it in
an unimaginable way.

00:16:44.036 --> 00:16:46.238
Do you, in many ways, feel
like everything that you just

00:16:46.238 --> 00:16:47.906
laid out in these few minutes

00:16:47.906 --> 00:16:50.075
sort of led you to that moment?

00:16:50.075 --> 00:16:52.945 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, when you got the call,
I mean were you just ready

00:16:52.945 --> 00:16:54.480
to be a leader in that moment?

00:16:56.215 --> 00:16:58.517
- Yes, strange to say, but yes.

00:16:58.517 --> 00:17:02.254
To make it even more direct,

00:17:02.254 --> 00:17:04.757 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had just finished
the first draft

00:17:04.757 --> 00:17:06.158
of a book called leadership.

00:17:07.593 --> 00:17:10.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
Somehow about a year before,
I'd been wanting to write

00:17:10.062 --> 00:17:11.997
a book forever and ever.

00:17:11.997 --> 00:17:13.966
I started three different books.

00:17:13.966 --> 00:17:15.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
I could never figure
out exactly what

00:17:15.434 --> 00:17:16.935
I wanted to write about.

00:17:16.935 --> 00:17:19.071
Somehow about a year before
I got the idea that I'd write

00:17:19.071 --> 00:17:20.539 align:start size:88% position:13%
about leadership because
I had been in a lot

00:17:20.539 --> 00:17:22.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
of different
leadership positions,

00:17:22.608 --> 00:17:24.843
both in government
and in business.

00:17:24.843 --> 00:17:26.145
I ran a coal mine once,

00:17:26.145 --> 00:17:27.913
which is all together
a different story.

00:17:27.913 --> 00:17:29.681
Got it out of bankruptcy.

00:17:29.681 --> 00:17:32.985 align:start position:0%
So I thought I would write
about how I was able to do that.

00:17:34.086 --> 00:17:35.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
At least, the
lessons that I used

00:17:35.687 --> 00:17:37.923
to teach myself a new business.

00:17:37.923 --> 00:17:41.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
I have a sort of
general feeling that

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:44.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'd rather have a great
manager than a subject matter

00:17:44.263 --> 00:17:47.399
expert in any situation.

00:17:47.399 --> 00:17:51.003
Because management
skills are sometimes

00:17:51.003 --> 00:17:54.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
harder to find than
subject matter expertise.

00:17:55.741 --> 00:17:59.978 align:start size:97% position:3%
The book talks about 20
lessons or so on leadership.

00:18:01.046 --> 00:18:03.082 align:start size:84% position:16%
So by the time
September 11 happened,

00:18:03.082 --> 00:18:05.551
it was in a final draft form.

00:18:05.551 --> 00:18:07.119
It was sitting in my desk.

00:18:07.119 --> 00:18:09.888
I was gonna publish it when
I left the mayor's office.

00:18:12.024 --> 00:18:14.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
Probably the day
before September 11,

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:15.861
I was pretty arrogant.

00:18:15.861 --> 00:18:16.862
If you had asked me, you know,

00:18:16.862 --> 00:18:18.564
could you handle an emergency?

00:18:18.564 --> 00:18:21.200
I'd say of course.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.235
I mean, I been the
mayor of New York.

00:18:23.235 --> 00:18:25.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a crisis of week,
it's an emergency of month

00:18:27.473 --> 00:18:30.542 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it's a catastrophic
emergency every six months.

00:18:30.542 --> 00:18:34.046
I mean, we had hurricanes,
we had airplane crashes,

00:18:34.046 --> 00:18:38.083
we had subway derailments,
we had hostage situations,

00:18:38.083 --> 00:18:39.384
we had terrorist attacks.

00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:44.923
I mean everyday
it was something.

00:18:44.923 --> 00:18:46.992
I had developed the
first mayor's office

00:18:46.992 --> 00:18:48.927
of emergency management

00:18:48.927 --> 00:18:51.196 align:start size:97% position:3%
because we had had a terrorist
attack the year before

00:18:51.196 --> 00:18:53.632
I came into office, in 1993.

00:18:53.632 --> 00:18:55.734 align:start size:94% position:6%
Probably another reason
that elected me is we were

00:18:55.734 --> 00:18:57.302
very poorly prepared for that.

00:18:59.505 --> 00:19:01.306 align:start size:84% position:16%
I established a
special mayor's office

00:19:01.306 --> 00:19:03.142
of emergency management.

00:19:03.142 --> 00:19:06.879
All they did was train us
on how to get ready for

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:10.149
nuclear attack, sarin gas,

00:19:10.149 --> 00:19:13.085 align:start size:81% position:19%
which was a big
problem back then,

00:19:14.553 --> 00:19:19.124
anthrax, which we actually did
get attacked with during 911.

00:19:19.124 --> 00:19:21.093
We went through West Nile virus.

00:19:21.093 --> 00:19:23.562 align:start size:97% position:3%
People don't remember that,
but it was kind of a mini-form

00:19:23.562 --> 00:19:25.664 line:0%
of the pandemic that
we just went through.

00:19:27.299 --> 00:19:28.534 line:0%
So we were very skilled.

00:19:28.534 --> 00:19:30.536 line:0%
When I say we, I
had a great team.

00:19:30.536 --> 00:19:32.738 line:0%
We were very skilled in
emergency management.

00:19:32.738 --> 00:19:34.973 line:0%
All of a sudden 911 happens,

00:19:34.973 --> 00:19:37.976
I arrive there, I
look at the scene.

00:19:39.411 --> 00:19:41.780
I see a man throw himself
out of the 100th floor.

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:44.183 align:start size:97% position:3%
I turned to my police
commissioner, Bernie Carey,

00:19:44.183 --> 00:19:47.085
and I say, "Bernie, we're
not prepared for this."

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
Whatever we did, this
challenges everything we know

00:19:50.956 --> 00:19:53.592
and we're gonna have to
use our instincts here.

00:19:53.592 --> 00:19:57.062
We don't have, we've got
24 plans for emergencies.

00:19:57.062 --> 00:19:58.897
We don't have one
that says airplanes

00:19:59.998 --> 00:20:03.035 align:start size:88% position:13%
crashing into
buildings like torpedo.

00:20:05.737 --> 00:20:08.507
We had to make it up
as we moved along.

00:20:08.507 --> 00:20:10.576
But the fact that we
had that experience,

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:13.579
kind of made it possible
for us to do that I think,

00:20:13.579 --> 00:20:15.814
a little better than
might have been done

00:20:15.814 --> 00:20:18.050
if we hadn't that experience.

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:19.918 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I know this is probably a
little bit of an odd question,

00:20:19.918 --> 00:20:24.122
but did you realize at
the time how important

00:20:24.122 --> 00:20:28.393
just sort of remaining, just
the optics of being in charge

00:20:28.393 --> 00:20:31.129
and being calm in it were?

00:20:31.129 --> 00:20:34.900
Because that, it felt like, I
mean, I remember being there,

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:37.202
it felt like the world was over

00:20:37.202 --> 00:20:39.571 align:start size:88% position:13%
or nothing even
remotely close to normal

00:20:39.571 --> 00:20:41.440
was ever gonna come back.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:43.442
Every time I turned
on CNN you were there

00:20:43.442 --> 00:20:45.510 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you were in the streets
or wherever you were

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:48.580 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it was like oh,
somebody's trying to make

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
some sense of this thing,
somebody's in charge.

00:20:52.951 --> 00:20:55.287
- Yeah, I knew that
on a smaller scale.

00:20:55.287 --> 00:20:59.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
I didn't realize the
enormity of it right away.

00:20:59.191 --> 00:21:02.094 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought of it, since I
had done so many emergencies

00:21:02.094 --> 00:21:04.363
in the city, it began for me as

00:21:06.064 --> 00:21:08.367
gigantic big, way beyond
anything we did before,

00:21:08.367 --> 00:21:10.602
but involving my city.

00:21:10.602 --> 00:21:12.537
But I mean, part of
handling an emergency

00:21:12.537 --> 00:21:15.741
is you control your emotions
and no matter how confused

00:21:15.741 --> 00:21:18.844
you are, no matter
how afraid you are,

00:21:18.844 --> 00:21:23.415
and no matter how upset you
are about somebody dying,

00:21:23.415 --> 00:21:24.349
you just control.

00:21:25.284 --> 00:21:26.585
You do...

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:30.022
I call it a Scarlett O'Hara.

00:21:30.022 --> 00:21:31.690
I'll think about it tomorrow.

00:21:32.858 --> 00:21:34.860
You know, I remember
when I was first told

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:37.062
that Father Judge died.

00:21:37.062 --> 00:21:40.232
He was the first person
whose body we found.

00:21:40.232 --> 00:21:43.969
Father Judge was my close
friend for eight years.

00:21:43.969 --> 00:21:46.672 align:start size:97% position:3%
He was the person that taught
me how to deal with death

00:21:46.672 --> 00:21:49.007 align:start size:84% position:16%
first time I lost
three fire fighters.

00:21:49.007 --> 00:21:51.443 align:start size:88% position:13%
He was an extraordinary
Franciscan priest.

00:21:53.979 --> 00:21:58.283 align:start size:88% position:13%
I found out that he died
almost simultaneously

00:21:58.283 --> 00:22:01.320
with my wanting to call my
priest fire commissioner

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:03.889
and say go get Father
Judge, I need him.

00:22:05.324 --> 00:22:07.793
Meaning, I needed him for
advice on how to explain

00:22:07.793 --> 00:22:10.829 align:start position:0%
to people, at that point we
thought 12,000 people were dead.

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:15.434 align:start size:97% position:3%
As I'm thinking that, my
fire commissioner walked in,

00:22:15.434 --> 00:22:19.671 align:start size:97% position:3%
Tom Von Essen, and he says,
"Mayor, we got a real problem.

00:22:21.006 --> 00:22:24.776 align:start position:0%
"They're carrying Father
Judge's body out of the rubble.

00:22:24.776 --> 00:22:26.611
"They're taking him over
to St. Peter's church."

00:22:26.611 --> 00:22:28.780
I said, "Is he dead?"

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:30.415
He said, "Yes."

00:22:30.415 --> 00:22:32.351
I would say, if
it hadn't been a,

00:22:34.186 --> 00:22:35.954 align:start size:97% position:3%
if I hadn't been in the
middle of it having to run it,

00:22:35.954 --> 00:22:37.022
I probably would
have gone in a room

00:22:37.022 --> 00:22:39.291
and cried for about 10 minutes,

00:22:39.291 --> 00:22:40.692
because he was so important.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:45.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I had to say to
myself, okay, contain it,

00:22:45.731 --> 00:22:50.736 align:start size:84% position:16%
stop it, you'll figure
this out tonight.

00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:53.205
You'll figure out
what he taught you

00:22:53.205 --> 00:22:55.173
and you'll use it tonight.

00:22:55.173 --> 00:22:59.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I had been trained like
that from earlier emergencies,

00:22:59.311 --> 00:23:00.545
that you just don't show it.

00:23:00.545 --> 00:23:01.346
You just,

00:23:03.248 --> 00:23:06.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
you put aside the emotion
and you think about

00:23:06.752 --> 00:23:09.688
what's the next practical
thing that you have to do

00:23:09.688 --> 00:23:12.457
in order to keep this
thing under control.

00:23:12.457 --> 00:23:13.959
- Yeah, and you did.

00:23:13.959 --> 00:23:15.894
We could obviously spend the
whole hour talking about this.

00:23:15.894 --> 00:23:17.162 align:start size:84% position:16%
I just have one
other thing on this

00:23:17.162 --> 00:23:19.498
and then we'll move on
to some other things.

00:23:19.498 --> 00:23:21.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, because right
now we live in such a time

00:23:21.566 --> 00:23:23.668 align:start size:88% position:13%
where it seems like
everyone's so polarized

00:23:23.668 --> 00:23:26.471
and so hating and all of that.

00:23:26.471 --> 00:23:28.407
You know, New York City has the

00:23:29.508 --> 00:23:31.476
idea that we all
hate each other.

00:23:31.476 --> 00:23:32.744
You know, I never
found New York City

00:23:32.744 --> 00:23:33.945
to be like that actually.

00:23:33.945 --> 00:23:35.113 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're kind of rough
on the outside,

00:23:35.113 --> 00:23:36.415 align:start size:84% position:16%
but if you ask anyone
for directions,

00:23:36.415 --> 00:23:38.283 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're happy to
talk to you usually.

00:23:38.283 --> 00:23:41.319
But I do remember for
a few months after 911

00:23:41.319 --> 00:23:44.990 align:start size:97% position:3%
there was like a really
special feeling on the street.

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:47.125
I remember actually, I told
you I lived on the Upper East

00:23:47.125 --> 00:23:49.928 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I went to play
basketball at the courts

00:23:49.928 --> 00:23:52.764 align:start size:88% position:13%
right by Gracie Mansion
on 90th and York.

00:23:52.764 --> 00:23:54.733
- Yeah I know them well.

00:23:54.733 --> 00:23:57.569
- Maybe two or three weeks
after 911 and we were playing

00:23:57.569 --> 00:24:00.539
basketball you could still
smell the soot in the air

00:24:00.539 --> 00:24:03.475
and all that and two
guys got into a fight

00:24:03.475 --> 00:24:05.710
and they were about
to punch each other.

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:08.814
Like, it was about to happen
and they both started crying.

00:24:08.814 --> 00:24:10.982
Basically, everyone on
the court started crying

00:24:10.982 --> 00:24:13.585
at the same, it all kind of
teared up at the same time.

00:24:13.585 --> 00:24:15.787 align:start size:88% position:13%
That there was something
so special about that

00:24:15.787 --> 00:24:17.022
and I wonder,

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:19.891
well can you just talk
about that feeling after

00:24:19.891 --> 00:24:21.793 align:start size:97% position:3%
and also do you think
there's a chance we can ever

00:24:21.793 --> 00:24:23.929
get back to something like that,

00:24:23.929 --> 00:24:26.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
for as crazy as
things seem right now?

00:24:26.097 --> 00:24:27.899 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I hope that's
still there, Dave.

00:24:27.899 --> 00:24:29.401
I hope so.

00:24:29.401 --> 00:24:32.871
It was an extraordinary,
I'd say three, four weeks.

00:24:35.307 --> 00:24:37.709 align:start size:81% position:19%
It was like the
world had changed.

00:24:37.709 --> 00:24:40.879
We were all friends,
by all friends I mean,

00:24:40.879 --> 00:24:42.814
Hillary Clinton
and Chuck Schumer

00:24:44.115 --> 00:24:45.484
and George Bush and

00:24:48.453 --> 00:24:51.156 align:start size:97% position:3%
the democratic congressman and
the republican congressmen.

00:24:51.156 --> 00:24:52.824
They came as a group.

00:24:52.824 --> 00:24:55.360
I took them on a
tour as a group.

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:57.496
These are people that
nowadays are all trying

00:24:57.496 --> 00:24:58.797
to put each other in jail.

00:25:00.765 --> 00:25:04.636 align:start position:0%
All they were interested in
was, legitimately interested in,

00:25:04.636 --> 00:25:07.639
is how can we help, how
can we get out of this,

00:25:09.241 --> 00:25:11.443
how can we learn from it?

00:25:11.443 --> 00:25:12.911
Very constructive.

00:25:12.911 --> 00:25:14.846
I mean, I got more offers
of help than I needed.

00:25:16.314 --> 00:25:19.150
Then I got two very, I got
two really terrific calls.

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:22.187
One from the governor
of Florida, Jeb Bush

00:25:22.187 --> 00:25:25.757 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the other from the mayor
of Chicago, Mayor Daley,

00:25:25.757 --> 00:25:29.294
and they both wanted to know
if they could send observers

00:25:30.662 --> 00:25:34.132 align:start position:0%
to watch so that they can
develop an emergency management

00:25:34.132 --> 00:25:37.335
office like we had for
when it happens to them.

00:25:37.335 --> 00:25:39.604
They didn't say if
it happens to them,

00:25:39.604 --> 00:25:41.439
instead, but when
it happens to them.

00:25:42.841 --> 00:25:45.877
I thought that was a very, very
intelligent decision to make

00:25:45.877 --> 00:25:47.279
looking into the future.

00:25:48.213 --> 00:25:49.614
Then a few others did that.

00:25:52.517 --> 00:25:55.053 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think, I've tried to think
of what's the difference

00:25:55.053 --> 00:26:00.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
between that and then
the pandemic, where
there's a certain

00:26:01.192 --> 00:26:02.727 align:start size:84% position:16%
amount of unity
that came out of it,

00:26:02.727 --> 00:26:06.264
but not for very long and
it quickly developed into

00:26:06.264 --> 00:26:08.633
almost a political thing.

00:26:08.633 --> 00:26:11.503
Into a political
thing, not almost.

00:26:11.503 --> 00:26:14.306
First of all, I think
the difference is that

00:26:15.674 --> 00:26:18.944 align:start size:94% position:6%
an attack on your country
by a specific group

00:26:18.944 --> 00:26:23.949
of foreigners or enemy, is
a very, very jarring thing

00:26:24.816 --> 00:26:26.251
that does bring you together.

00:26:26.251 --> 00:26:29.721 align:start position:0%
It's like somebody, you're
feuding with your family members

00:26:29.721 --> 00:26:32.290
and you're all at home fighting

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:34.893 align:start position:0%
and then all of a sudden people
want to attack your house

00:26:34.893 --> 00:26:37.729
and take the entire house
from the entire family.

00:26:37.729 --> 00:26:39.331
I think you get over the feuding

00:26:40.532 --> 00:26:42.033
when you're fighting
for the same cause.

00:26:42.033 --> 00:26:45.604
I think a thing like the
pandemic is more diffused.

00:26:45.604 --> 00:26:48.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
There was so many different
explanations for it.

00:26:49.507 --> 00:26:51.209
There was so much
confusion about it

00:26:51.209 --> 00:26:52.644
and contradiction about it.

00:26:54.079 --> 00:26:57.115
Then right away the attempt
to make political gain on it.

00:26:58.149 --> 00:27:00.251
It didn't take a week or so,

00:27:00.251 --> 00:27:01.987
of course I see if
from republican eyes,

00:27:01.987 --> 00:27:06.758
but for Schumer and Pelosi to
say that Trump wasn't handling

00:27:06.758 --> 00:27:11.329
it right and he didn't
pick it up right away.

00:27:11.329 --> 00:27:14.332 align:start size:97% position:3%
Of course, ignoring the fact
that they didn't either.

00:27:14.332 --> 00:27:15.934
He picked it up before they did.

00:27:17.035 --> 00:27:19.571 line:0%
She was inviting
people to go out

00:27:19.571 --> 00:27:22.674 line:0%
and the idiot mayor here in
the city for two weeks after

00:27:22.674 --> 00:27:24.843 line:0%
Trump declared it
was an emergency,

00:27:24.843 --> 00:27:26.311 line:0%
was telling people to go out.

00:27:27.646 --> 00:27:30.048 align:start size:97% position:3%
So there was plenty of
political blame to go around.

00:27:30.048 --> 00:27:33.685
September 11, there probably
was that political blame also

00:27:33.685 --> 00:27:37.856
but everybody stopped it and
said you know, it's stupid.

00:27:39.057 --> 00:27:40.592
All that political
blame was stupid.

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:45.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
Pelosi didn't cause the
pandemic and neither did Trump

00:27:45.597 --> 00:27:47.732
and neither did
Schumer or Cuomo.

00:27:47.732 --> 00:27:49.334
China did.

00:27:49.334 --> 00:27:53.938
China caused it and then
we all reacted to something

00:27:53.938 --> 00:27:55.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
that was extraordinary,
something new,

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:56.808
something we didn't know about.

00:27:56.808 --> 00:27:59.110 align:start size:88% position:13%
How could they have
reacted to it properly?

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:01.046 align:start size:84% position:16%
They didn't know
what this virus was.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:04.382
How was Fauci, everyone
says well Fauci had about

00:28:04.382 --> 00:28:05.216
three different views on it.

00:28:05.216 --> 00:28:06.618
Well, he did.

00:28:06.618 --> 00:28:08.520
But he didn't know what it was.

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:10.855
He may be a genius
but he's not God.

00:28:12.223 --> 00:28:14.459
Same thing with September 11.

00:28:14.459 --> 00:28:16.327 align:start size:84% position:16%
People would criticize
the reaction to it

00:28:16.327 --> 00:28:19.798 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'd say, geez, everybody's
doing the best they can.

00:28:19.798 --> 00:28:22.467
This is something we
never faced before.

00:28:22.467 --> 00:28:25.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you know, I think we're
doing a pretty good job

00:28:25.403 --> 00:28:27.839
for something we
didn't face before.

00:28:27.839 --> 00:28:29.641
That existed with September 11.

00:28:29.641 --> 00:28:30.909
It doesn't exist now.

00:28:30.909 --> 00:28:33.845
We go after the pettiest
little thing right away.

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:38.917
- Yeah, it's a pretty scary
premise that what we need

00:28:38.917 --> 00:28:42.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
potentially to bring us
together is the unspeakable.

00:28:43.388 --> 00:28:45.690
But you know, a few
years after you left

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:48.626
and you were no longer mayor,
I don't think a lot of people

00:28:48.626 --> 00:28:51.529
remember that you actually
were gonna run for senator

00:28:51.529 --> 00:28:54.032
or you started running
for senator from New York

00:28:54.032 --> 00:28:56.801 align:start size:88% position:13%
against Hillary Clinton
and then you were--

00:28:56.801 --> 00:28:58.236
- I was gonna run.

00:28:58.236 --> 00:29:00.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, you were diagnosed
with prostate cancer

00:29:00.705 --> 00:29:04.209 align:start position:0%
and then Rick Lasio, who I think
was just a local politician

00:29:04.209 --> 00:29:05.543
in Long Island, if
I'm not mistaken,

00:29:05.543 --> 00:29:07.946 align:start size:84% position:16%
he stepped in and
Hillary obviously won.

00:29:07.946 --> 00:29:09.414 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Rick was the
member of congress,

00:29:09.414 --> 00:29:10.482
he was a member of congress.

00:29:10.482 --> 00:29:11.549
- Member of congress.

00:29:12.817 --> 00:29:15.487 align:start size:88% position:13%
But obviously, he
didn't make major waves,

00:29:15.487 --> 00:29:17.956
where I think with
you and Hillary,

00:29:17.956 --> 00:29:19.224 align:start size:81% position:19%
it would have been
a real battle.

00:29:19.224 --> 00:29:20.725
- Oh, it'd a been a
great race. (laughs)

00:29:20.725 --> 00:29:22.794
- Yeah, do you ever
think about that,

00:29:22.794 --> 00:29:26.331
that regardless of whether
you would have won or lost,

00:29:26.331 --> 00:29:29.200 align:start size:88% position:13%
that history could
have absolutely changed

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:30.769
because of that election.

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:34.906
- It could have, I mean it
could have changed, sure.

00:29:34.906 --> 00:29:37.308
I mean, yes.

00:29:38.176 --> 00:29:39.511
Sure you think about it and

00:29:41.279 --> 00:29:44.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I'm not, I don't
dwell a lot on the things

00:29:44.082 --> 00:29:45.049
that didn't happen.

00:29:46.251 --> 00:29:47.652
I sort of dwell on the
things that did happen

00:29:47.652 --> 00:29:48.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
and did they happen
right or wrong

00:29:48.853 --> 00:29:50.989 align:start size:88% position:13%
and if they happened
wrong, how do we adjust.

00:29:50.989 --> 00:29:54.025 align:start position:0%
So it happened, it was something
that I wasn't meant to do

00:29:56.861 --> 00:29:58.596
and then I did run for president

00:29:58.596 --> 00:30:00.965
to try to run against her again.

00:30:00.965 --> 00:30:02.367
I lost and she lost.

00:30:02.367 --> 00:30:04.669
So we never did get to
run against each other.

00:30:06.004 --> 00:30:07.005
- Do you regret,

00:30:08.506 --> 00:30:09.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
I guess I don't even
have to ask this question

00:30:09.741 --> 00:30:11.743
'cause you don't dwell
on things, but you know,

00:30:11.743 --> 00:30:14.145 align:start size:97% position:3%
you sort of had this strategy
that you were gonna wait

00:30:14.145 --> 00:30:16.314
sort of, for the big
stuff till Florida

00:30:16.314 --> 00:30:18.149
and then it just didn't happen.

00:30:18.149 --> 00:30:20.018
But I guess I don't have
to ask you if you regret it

00:30:20.018 --> 00:30:21.519
because you don't dwell.

00:30:21.519 --> 00:30:23.021
- No, I don't regret it.

00:30:23.021 --> 00:30:25.190
I think, I actually think
when I look back on it,

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:26.858
two things make it easy.

00:30:26.858 --> 00:30:30.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
One, I think the strategy
would not have worked

00:30:30.295 --> 00:30:31.863
no matter how we did it.

00:30:31.863 --> 00:30:36.000 align:start position:0%
I was too moderate a republican
for them to nominate.

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:40.138
I'm the last nominee to try
to become the republican

00:30:40.138 --> 00:30:43.041
candidate for president
who's at all pro-choice.

00:30:44.609 --> 00:30:46.477
I'm you know, three
months pro-choice.

00:30:48.746 --> 00:30:51.516
I'm one of those people who
is pro-life individually,

00:30:52.717 --> 00:30:56.354
but I am willing to
say that other people

00:30:56.354 --> 00:30:58.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
have different moral
views about this

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:00.758
and a woman should have
a three month period

00:31:00.758 --> 00:31:03.127 align:start size:81% position:19%
in which to decide
whether or not

00:31:04.729 --> 00:31:06.531
she can have the baby.

00:31:06.531 --> 00:31:09.500
I don't like that, I
wish it were different.

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:12.003
I offer, when I was
mayor, I would offer

00:31:13.304 --> 00:31:15.106
and the other side
would get very angry,

00:31:15.106 --> 00:31:17.809
offer adoption as
an alternative.

00:31:17.809 --> 00:31:20.345 align:start size:97% position:3%
But then ultimately, I think
it's the woman's choice

00:31:20.345 --> 00:31:23.781 align:start size:97% position:3%
and finally, I can't find
it in my heart to put people

00:31:23.781 --> 00:31:27.352
in jail for it, which is the
only way you can enforce it.

00:31:27.352 --> 00:31:30.021
Now, the whole abortion
debate has become now,

00:31:30.021 --> 00:31:32.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
like crazy, like
everything else has.

00:31:32.257 --> 00:31:35.426
Course, now we're talking about
abortion in the eighth month

00:31:36.427 --> 00:31:38.630
and we have this crazy governor

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:43.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
in Virginia who is
talking about abortion

00:31:43.167 --> 00:31:44.369
after the baby's born.

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:47.138
I don't know if
he's just stupid.

00:31:47.138 --> 00:31:48.873
Yeah, I don't know if he's
stupid and he didn't know

00:31:48.873 --> 00:31:51.776 align:start size:94% position:6%
what he said is too
stubborn to go back on it.

00:31:51.776 --> 00:31:54.679
What he really means is that
you should get a waiting period

00:31:54.679 --> 00:31:56.681
so that the doctor and
the mother can talk over

00:31:56.681 --> 00:31:58.483
what to do with the baby.

00:31:59.884 --> 00:32:01.619
There's nothing you
can do with the baby

00:32:01.619 --> 00:32:03.087
except commit murder, right.

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:06.224
- Yeah, well it's interesting

00:32:06.224 --> 00:32:07.492
and I'm glad you mentioned that.

00:32:07.492 --> 00:32:10.028
I know you read my book,
I take the 12 week also.

00:32:10.028 --> 00:32:13.231
So the three month position
and people will say to me,

00:32:13.231 --> 00:32:15.633 align:start size:97% position:3%
well Dave, you seem to
align with the conservatives

00:32:15.633 --> 00:32:17.101
on so many issues.

00:32:17.101 --> 00:32:19.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is the one that you don't
really align with them on.

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:20.471
I'm actually fine with that

00:32:21.673 --> 00:32:23.308
because I always find
that the conservatives

00:32:23.308 --> 00:32:26.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
that disagree with us on
this, they're always willing

00:32:26.311 --> 00:32:27.745
to talk about it respectfully.

00:32:27.745 --> 00:32:30.548 align:start position:0%
Do you ever find that they
won't talk about it respectfully

00:32:30.548 --> 00:32:32.083
versus on the other side,

00:32:32.083 --> 00:32:34.152
where if you don't
agree, you're done?

00:32:35.353 --> 00:32:37.121
- Yeah, I mean,

00:32:37.121 --> 00:32:38.656
I'm not sure it was
always that way,

00:32:38.656 --> 00:32:42.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the way it is broken down
now and I hope this isn't

00:32:42.527 --> 00:32:45.496
my own prejudice, I
find far more ability

00:32:45.496 --> 00:32:49.734 align:start size:97% position:3%
within the conservative
movement, republican movement,

00:32:49.734 --> 00:32:53.171 align:start size:97% position:3%
to talk about our
disagreements without anger

00:32:54.238 --> 00:32:56.007
than on the democratic side.

00:32:56.007 --> 00:32:57.875
For example, when I was running,

00:32:57.875 --> 00:32:59.143
Mike Huckabee and I were running

00:32:59.143 --> 00:33:01.479
against each other
and after one debate,

00:33:01.479 --> 00:33:04.682
I think it was the 3rd or 4th
debate and we're good friends,

00:33:04.682 --> 00:33:07.318 align:start position:0%
he said to me, "You know mayor,
I'm a minister and I have

00:33:07.318 --> 00:33:11.656
"trouble, even with your
narrow pro-choice position.

00:33:11.656 --> 00:33:13.558
"It would be hard for
me if I dropped out

00:33:13.558 --> 00:33:15.727
"to endorse you in the primary."

00:33:15.727 --> 00:33:19.130
Although I have to think,
he gave me some reasons why

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:22.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
he thought maybe I was the
best candidate other than him.

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:24.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
I said, "Well Mike, I'm very
impressed with you too."

00:33:24.302 --> 00:33:27.438
He said, "And the reason
I would endorse you is

00:33:27.438 --> 00:33:29.807
"because you're
honest about it."

00:33:29.807 --> 00:33:33.711 align:start size:97% position:3%
He contrasted me with Romney,
