00:39:29.500 --> 00:39:31.268 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Similar experience.
- New York City.

00:39:33.537 --> 00:39:36.774 align:start position:0%
- You know, when I was a
prosecutor for many, many years,

00:39:36.774 --> 00:39:38.309
16, 17, 18 years,

00:39:38.309 --> 00:39:41.612
I was a federal prosecutor,
which meant I always tried

00:39:41.612 --> 00:39:43.314
very big cases.

00:39:43.314 --> 00:39:46.384
My cases, you don't
try the small cases.

00:39:46.384 --> 00:39:50.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
As a local prosecutor,
you'll try the murder case

00:39:50.087 --> 00:39:52.556
and the theft case.

00:39:52.556 --> 00:39:55.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
Federal prosecutor, you're
trying organized crime,

00:39:55.426 --> 00:39:58.763
you're trying very, very
intricate financial fraud.

00:39:59.964 --> 00:40:03.401
High enough levels so it
arises to federal crime.

00:40:03.401 --> 00:40:08.406
I was not a natural proponent
of the broken windows theory.

00:40:09.273 --> 00:40:10.207
It didn't even make sense to me

00:40:10.207 --> 00:40:11.709
when it was first told to me.

00:40:11.709 --> 00:40:13.711
Because I always thought
we paid too much attention

00:40:13.711 --> 00:40:17.314 align:start position:0%
to the small things, not enough
attention to the big crime.

00:40:17.314 --> 00:40:18.549
It was taught to me by

00:40:20.017 --> 00:40:22.820
about 1981, when I was the
associate attorney general

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:24.188
of the United States.

00:40:24.188 --> 00:40:27.525 align:start size:97% position:3%
We established a commission
on violent crime.

00:40:27.525 --> 00:40:30.227
Professor James Q. Wilson
was the vice chair of it.

00:40:31.562 --> 00:40:34.698 align:start size:97% position:3%
I got very close to Professor
Wilson, who developed

00:40:34.698 --> 00:40:37.334
this theory at Harvard
with Professor Kelling.

00:40:38.536 --> 00:40:40.704
What he tried to
explain to me was,

00:40:40.704 --> 00:40:43.808
first of all, you're not
an expert on street crime.

00:40:43.808 --> 00:40:45.075
I'm gonna make you one.

00:40:45.075 --> 00:40:47.111
You're an expert on
high level crime,

00:40:47.111 --> 00:40:50.181
even mafia crime is
high level crime.

00:40:50.181 --> 00:40:52.116
Let's take a look
at street crime,

00:40:52.116 --> 00:40:54.018 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's what you
did for two years.

00:40:54.018 --> 00:40:58.022
He said an awful lot
of it has to do with

00:40:58.022 --> 00:41:01.625
what the young people
see in the community.

00:41:03.093 --> 00:41:04.995
He said, let's make
it really simple.

00:41:04.995 --> 00:41:09.700 align:start position:0%
If they see a lawful community,
where the expectation is

00:41:09.700 --> 00:41:13.504 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you don't ruin other
people's property by putting

00:41:13.504 --> 00:41:17.675
graffiti all over it, that
you have to pay when you go

00:41:17.675 --> 00:41:21.712
through the turnstile, if you
take something from someone

00:41:21.712 --> 00:41:25.282 align:start position:0%
you have to give it back or
there are punishments for that.

00:41:26.817 --> 00:41:29.653
He said if society
is based on that,

00:41:29.653 --> 00:41:32.223
you have very little big crime.

00:41:32.223 --> 00:41:33.724
Reverse it.

00:41:33.724 --> 00:41:37.061
If the society looks
like your New York,

00:41:37.061 --> 00:41:38.963
where everything is
filled with graffiti,

00:41:38.963 --> 00:41:42.433
as if somebody's property
doesn't mean anything,

00:41:42.433 --> 00:41:45.769
where you don't have to
pay for the turnstile,

00:41:45.769 --> 00:41:47.972
where you can spit
on the sidewalk,

00:41:47.972 --> 00:41:50.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
where you can urinate
in the street,

00:41:50.140 --> 00:41:52.076
where you can do
anything you want,

00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:55.613
the attitude begins
at a very early age

00:41:55.613 --> 00:41:58.716
that there are no limits,
there are restriction.

00:41:59.917 --> 00:42:01.385
By the time they start
to get to the big crime,

00:42:01.385 --> 00:42:03.320 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's really too
hard to deal with.

00:42:04.889 --> 00:42:06.991
I even translated
that into my theory

00:42:06.991 --> 00:42:10.594
of how you stop a riot, which
they failed to do this time

00:42:10.594 --> 00:42:12.997
and we never failed to do it.

00:42:12.997 --> 00:42:16.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
You stop a riot
before it is a riot.

00:42:16.867 --> 00:42:18.536
You anticipate.

00:42:18.536 --> 00:42:22.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
You see a protest,
wonderful things protests.

00:42:22.907 --> 00:42:25.910
People have a right to call
me names, call you names,

00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:27.845
whatever they want to do.

00:42:27.845 --> 00:42:29.914 align:start size:81% position:19%
But I used to give
them a lecture,

00:42:29.914 --> 00:42:32.750
'cause Dinkins had
so many protests,

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:34.885
I just had to stop it cold.

00:42:34.885 --> 00:42:36.687
I wasn't gonna have any.

00:42:36.687 --> 00:42:40.357
So I gave a speech and I said,
"Let's understand each other.

00:42:40.357 --> 00:42:42.526
"You can have all the
protests you want.

00:42:42.526 --> 00:42:44.695
"I'm pro-first amendment.

00:42:44.695 --> 00:42:46.897
"You can do anything
you want protesting,

00:42:46.897 --> 00:42:50.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
"however, the minute
you throw a rock

00:42:50.167 --> 00:42:51.869
"you're gonna go to jail.

00:42:51.869 --> 00:42:53.137
"You're not gonna get arrested,

00:42:53.137 --> 00:42:55.339
"you're gonna go
to Riker's Island.

00:42:55.339 --> 00:42:57.374
"I'll let you sit there for
a day before I even take you

00:42:57.374 --> 00:42:58.208
"before a judge.

00:42:59.643 --> 00:43:03.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
"If you scratch a car,
you're gonna get arrested.

00:43:03.581 --> 00:43:05.849
"If you burn anything, I'm
gonna try to put you in jail

00:43:05.849 --> 00:43:07.351
"for a long time.

00:43:07.351 --> 00:43:09.153 align:start size:84% position:16%
"And if you physically
harm someone,

00:43:10.988 --> 00:43:13.591 align:start size:88% position:13%
"we're talking five,
six, seven, eight years.

00:43:13.591 --> 00:43:16.460
"And if you're a juvenile, I
won't treat you as a juvenile.

00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:17.661
"Treat you as an adult."

00:43:19.029 --> 00:43:20.898 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, I'm gonna be really,
really nice to you if you have

00:43:20.898 --> 00:43:22.266
a peaceful protest.

00:43:22.266 --> 00:43:24.001
I'm gonna protect you.

00:43:24.001 --> 00:43:25.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm gonna make it
easy for you to do it

00:43:25.769 --> 00:43:28.072
and I don't really
care what you say.

00:43:28.072 --> 00:43:30.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can call me a Nazi, you
can call me whatever you want.

00:43:32.109 --> 00:43:35.479 align:start size:88% position:13%
But it isn't be the
last act of criminality.

00:43:35.479 --> 00:43:37.414
It's the first one
we're gonna go after

00:43:37.414 --> 00:43:40.818
because David Dinkins had
a theory he called venting

00:43:40.818 --> 00:43:42.853
and it caused the Crown Heights

00:43:42.853 --> 00:43:44.989 align:start size:81% position:19%
and the Washington
Heights riots.

00:43:44.989 --> 00:43:46.924
They let it go on
for three days.

00:43:48.359 --> 00:43:52.029
By the time Ray Kelly had to
solve it in Crown Heights,

00:43:52.029 --> 00:43:54.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
many, many Jewish people,
one had been killed,

00:43:54.398 --> 00:43:57.568
many had been beaten
with kind of a pogrom.

00:43:57.568 --> 00:43:59.970
They did the same thing
in Washington Heights.

00:43:59.970 --> 00:44:01.772
That's essentially
what happened to us

00:44:02.840 --> 00:44:05.075
in all those democratic cities.

00:44:05.075 --> 00:44:07.211
In Minnesota, they didn't
enforce the law for three

00:44:07.211 --> 00:44:08.479
or four days.

00:44:08.479 --> 00:44:11.649
In New York, I mean I
have pictures of people

00:44:11.649 --> 00:44:14.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
going into stores, walking
out with television,

00:44:14.785 --> 00:44:17.721
walking out with liquor,
the cops are watching.

00:44:19.323 --> 00:44:21.992
Cops are not observers of crime.

00:44:21.992 --> 00:44:24.294 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're not there
to record the crime.

00:44:24.294 --> 00:44:26.296
They're there to stop it.

00:44:26.296 --> 00:44:27.698
- Can you talk a
little bit about

00:44:27.698 --> 00:44:30.801 align:start size:97% position:3%
the tension between the cops?
- The more difficulty.

00:44:30.801 --> 00:44:32.236
They had exactly--

00:44:32.236 --> 00:44:36.740 align:start size:97% position:3%
I maintain that these riots,
in part were caused by

00:44:38.075 --> 00:44:42.546 align:start size:97% position:3%
the boy mayor of Minneapolis,
who let them take over

00:44:42.546 --> 00:44:43.547
the police precinct.

